WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.331 --> 00:00:03.091
(relaxing music)

00:00:03.091 --> 00:00:04.660
- I live in Palo Alto California,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.251
and it's a bizarre thing, I think,

00:00:06.251 --> 00:00:09.651
for people here to build
things, physical things.

00:00:09.651 --> 00:00:12.749
Uh, most folks are doing dot-coms.

00:00:12.749 --> 00:00:14.340
It's kind of Neverland in Palo Alto,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.280
and so anything is possible.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.949
My name is Chris Robinson,

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:19.678
and for four or five years

00:00:19.678 --> 00:00:22.296
I've been working on
building the Tsunami ball.

00:00:22.296 --> 00:00:25.879
It's designed to survive
an inland tsunami.

00:00:30.554 --> 00:00:33.361
March 2011, there was an earthquake

00:00:33.361 --> 00:00:35.298
off the shore off of Sendai in Japan,

00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:37.886
which caused a tsunami to basically

00:00:37.886 --> 00:00:40.563
smash against the eastern seaboard.

00:00:40.563 --> 00:00:42.057
When the tsunami happened,

00:00:42.057 --> 00:00:44.282
I was at this incubator in Mountain View,

00:00:44.282 --> 00:00:47.572
and I was watching live footage
of people trying to escape.

00:00:47.572 --> 00:00:50.456
And the entire office
just starts discussing

00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:52.446
how they would fix that or solve that.

00:00:52.446 --> 00:00:55.878
And I decided then, well,
you could very simply

00:00:55.878 --> 00:00:58.076
just put them in like a ping pong ball.

00:00:58.076 --> 00:00:59.844
Like, a sphere is a very strong shape,

00:00:59.844 --> 00:01:01.623
and so you could usher your family into,

00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:02.910
and they would float around

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.211
and they would be safe.

00:01:05.211 --> 00:01:08.344
And so I started just kind
of modifying the designs,

00:01:08.344 --> 00:01:11.399
and at some point, I just
extruded the shape of it

00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:13.316
and now it's a capsule.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.283
Almost everything I'm doing
here is brand new to me.

00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:18.276
I made mistakes from day one.

00:01:18.276 --> 00:01:20.592
And this was this piece
that I custom designed

00:01:20.592 --> 00:01:21.670
to be super strong,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.386
and it's massively thick, right,

00:01:23.386 --> 00:01:24.374
it's four inches think.

00:01:24.374 --> 00:01:27.144
But yeah, it twisted and
just snapped in half.

00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:29.648
So this is what happened
when the boat fell on me.

00:01:29.648 --> 00:01:32.199
You just hope that you survive
the mistakes long enough

00:01:32.199 --> 00:01:34.372
so that you can make progress,

00:01:34.372 --> 00:01:36.289
um, and then you can, you can learn.

00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:44.875
The idea is that we'll
have electric motors,

00:01:44.875 --> 00:01:47.358
solar panels on the outside and batteries,

00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:49.244
and you'll be able to
navigate from the boat.

00:01:49.244 --> 00:01:50.686
So it's not gonna be a speed boat,

00:01:50.686 --> 00:01:52.362
but you'd be able to navigate your way

00:01:52.362 --> 00:01:53.220
wherever you need to go,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.614
back to shore or help
somebody else that needs help.

00:01:56.614 --> 00:01:58.954
So we kind of share the same ideas

00:01:58.954 --> 00:02:01.592
about how to evaluate someone's project,

00:02:01.592 --> 00:02:05.466
and it's usually based on
intelligence or money, time.

00:02:05.466 --> 00:02:06.508
And on those three things,

00:02:06.508 --> 00:02:09.160
this is the dumbest
project you could ever do.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.563
And so I just think of it as an adventure.

00:02:11.563 --> 00:02:14.055
I'm excited every day that
I'm out here working on it.

00:02:14.055 --> 00:02:16.972
The importance is just building it.

00:02:21.929 --> 00:02:24.679
(chiming music)

